{"id":3719,"date":"2021-09-16T14:01:13","date_gmt":"2021-09-16T21:01:13","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3719"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:48","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:48","slug":"2-5-conditionals","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-5-conditionals\/","title":{"rendered":"2.5 Conditionals"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a0ed\" name=\"6a2214715a0ed\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain what a conditional is and how it is used to change a program&#8217;s flow<\/li>\n<li>Explain the order that a conditional uses to evaluate its statements<\/li>\n<li>Describe how Python uses indentation to identify parts of a program<\/li>\n<li>Create an if \/ else conditional<\/li>\n<li>Identify the parts of a conditional<\/li>\n<li>Explain the importance of order of conditional statements<\/li>\n<li>Create a conditional with an elif (else if) condition<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What are If Statements?<\/li>\n<li>Use the Tab Key: The Importance of Indentation<\/li>\n<li>Creating a Condition<\/li>\n<li>Advanced Conditionals: The elif<\/li>\n<li>Who Needs Else?<\/li>\n<li>The If Order Ordeal<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Rollercoaster<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Different Altitudes, Different Attitudes<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a341\" name=\"6a2214715a341\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are if statements?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If statements are conditionals, which are used to help your program make decisions. You actually use conditionals every day! Some examples might include:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If my alarm goes off, then I will wake up. Else, I will stay asleep.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If I am hungry, I will eat something.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If it&#8217;s sunny out, I will wear sunscreen.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If my room is clean, I will get my allowance. Else, I will make no money.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Watch the video below to learn more about conditionals:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3719-1\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/conditionals1-codrone.mp4?_=1\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/conditionals1-codrone.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/conditionals1-codrone.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Some important rules to remember:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>If tests a condition ONCE. If the condition is true, it will do the steps indented under the If. Otherwise, it goes on to the next If or Else.<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u201cElse\u201d has no condition. This is because \u201celse\u201d is the last option when using if-else statements.\u00a0 When a computer reaches the \u201celse\u201d option after checking all the other conditions, the else condition is automatically true and will run its statements.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">One way to use \u201celse\u201d would be when something needs to happen when a condition is false. When the if condition is false and nothing needs to happen, you don\u2019t need to use an \u201celse\u201d.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a3db\" name=\"6a2214715a3db\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Use the Tab Key: The Importance of Indentation<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Indentation is very important in Python. The indentations indicate which statements belong to a particular section in the code.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You may have noticed that the statements below the if and else statements are indented <\/span><b>4 spaces<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> or one <\/span><b>tab<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. If you want to have multiple statements executed if a condition is true using proper indentation, it would look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if battery_percentage &gt; 60:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Ready for takeoff!\")\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.land()\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0print(\"Battery too low.\")<\/pre>\n<p>Below is an incorrect example. Try running it to see what happens. Your goal: correct the example by indenting correctly and fixing the syntax.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drones = 100\r\n\r\nbugs = 55\r\n\r\nif drones &gt; bugs print(\"Squish the bugs!\")\r\n\r\nelse print(\"Fly away!\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The example should look something like this. Try running it now and change the values of drones and bugs.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drones = 100\r\n\r\nbugs = 55\r\n\r\nif drones &gt; bugs:\r\n\r\n    print(\"Squish the bugs!\")\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Fly away!\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a43c\" name=\"6a2214715a43c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a Condition<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create 3 variables with different numerical values. Next, write at least 3 if statements and an else statement. Use the variables as the conditions for the if statements. The statements of the if statements and else statement should print different strings. The program below is an example &#8212; try writing your own!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n \r\nx = 58\r\ny = 12\r\nz = 90\r\n \r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\nif x &gt; y:\r\n   drone.set_pitch(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif z &gt; x:\r\n   drone.set_roll(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif y+z &gt; x:\r\n   drone.set_yaw(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nif y &gt; z:\r\n   drone.set_throttle(50)\r\n   drone.move(2)\r\nelse:\r\n   drone.land()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a4fd\" name=\"6a2214715a4fd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Advanced Conditionals: The elif<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]We have seen that standard conditionals with an IF and ELSE will give us 2 possible outcomes. But what if we want to have 3 or more possible outcomes? Watch the video below to learn more about how we can make this happen:<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3719-2\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-1-Codrone.mp4?_=2\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-1-Codrone.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-1-Codrone.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]So what are the rules with elif (pronounced else if)?<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The first IF is evaluated. If it is true, it does this and skips the rest.<\/li>\n<li>If the first IF is False, the elif is evaluated. If true, it does the steps for the elif and skips the rest.<\/li>\n<li>If both the IF and ELIF are False, the else steps are executed.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a5b9\" name=\"6a2214715a5b9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Who Needs Else?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we know the primary parts of a conditional, what happens when we don&#8217;t have all those parts? Can a conditional not have an else or not have an elif? Watch the video below to find out!<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3719-3\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/advanced-conditionals-no-else.mp4?_=3\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/advanced-conditionals-no-else.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/advanced-conditionals-no-else.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we know we don&#8217;t need an elif or else, let&#8217;s think of some examples where we might want just an if statement. Come up with 5 examples where you might need only an if.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a6e9\" name=\"6a2214715a6e9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>The If Order Ordeal<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Does the order of statements in a conditional matter? Copy the code below and then watch the video to follow along!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">battery_power = 50\r\nif(battery_power &lt;40):\r\n    print(\"battery is getting low\")\r\nelif(battery_power &lt;20):\r\n    print(\"battery level critical\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"battery normal\")<\/pre>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3719-4\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-Order-of-Conditionals.mp4?_=4\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-Order-of-Conditionals.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/09\/Advanced-Conditionals-Order-of-Conditionals.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we know order is important, try to find the error in the following code:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height = 35\r\nif height &gt; 20:\r\n     print(\"Ready for movements!\")\r\nif height &gt; 40:\r\n     print(\"Altitude limit exceeded!\")\r\nelif height &gt; 30: \r\n     print (\"Warning! Approaching altitude limit\")<\/pre>\n<p>Additionally, Answer the following questions about the code above:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>How many conditionals are there?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 35?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 15?<\/li>\n<li>What happens if your height variable is 25?<\/li>\n<li>What would you do to fix the error in this code?<\/li>\n<li>Will the program still run with the code as it is shown above?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Now, create and run your own code that has an ordering error in the conditional. Demonstrate how different values can return unexpected or unwanted results.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214715a7a8\" name=\"6a2214715a7a8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Rollercoaster Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This challenge will be checking if a person is tall enough to ride a rollercoaster. <\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You\u2019ll declare a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">, which will represent height in inches. <\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The height should be checked at 48 and 36. <\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Anyone under 36 inches is not allowed to ride. <\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Riders between 36 and 48 inches can ride with a parent. <\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Riders above 48 inches can ride alone.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2846&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Add the drone in by having it throttle up for 3 seconds at 48 inches and above and at 2 seconds for 36-48 inches. For any variable under 36 inches, it should yaw for 1 second.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nheight = 66\u00a0 # height in inches\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\r\nif height &gt; 48:\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(3)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Rider can ride alone\")\r\n\r\nelif height &gt; 36:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Rider must be accompanied by parent\")\r\n\r\nelse:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_yaw(50)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Rider not allowed on the ride\")\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>drone.close()[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<a id=\"6a2214715a820\" name=\"6a2214715a820\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Different Altitudes, Different Attitudes Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This challenge will be checking our drone&#8217;s height and then having it move in different ways based on the height value.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You\u2019ll declare a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">, which will represent height in inches.<\/span><\/li>\n<li>Have height be a random value between 10 and 40<\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Check the height variable and have your drone do the following:<\/span><\/li>\n<li>If the height is under 10, increase the drone&#8217;s height for 1 second<\/li>\n<li>If the height is between 10 and 20, have the drone slide to the right for 1 second<\/li>\n<li>If the height is between 20 and 30, have the drone slide to the left for 1 second<\/li>\n<li>If the height is between 30 and 40, have the drone fly forward for 1 second<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Explain what a conditional is and how it is used&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-5-conditionals\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4152,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2119,2123,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-3719","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-level-2","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3719","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":7,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3719\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4364,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3719\/revisions\/4364"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4152"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3719"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3719"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3719"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}